 "result": "**Mechanism:** a `ContextVar` plus werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects, all in `src/tessel/globals.py`.\n\n- `_app_var` (`globals.py:40`) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` holding the current context.\n- `app_ctx` (`globals.py:41`) is a `LocalProxy` onto `_app_var` itself.\n- `active_app` (`globals.py:44`) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n- `g` (`globals.py:47`) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.g`, the `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n- `request` (`globals.py:57`) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.request`, the `Request` from `wrappers.py`. `session` works the same way.\n\n**Who sets it:** `AppContext` in `src/tessel/ctx.py:260`. `push()` calls `_app_var.set(self)` and stores the token in `_cv_token` (`ctx.py:433`). `pop()` calls `_app_var.reset(token)` (`ctx.py:498`).\n\n**Lookup:** each proxy reads `_app_var` at access time and then takes the named attribute. Because it is a `ContextVar`, the result is per-thread and per-async-task. If no context is pushed, the proxy raises the `unbound_message` error, \"Working outside of application context\" or \"Working outside of request context\".\n\nRequest and app contexts are merged into a single `AppContext`. `request_ctx` is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx` (`globals.py:65-75`).",
